.TH msg "" "Command"
.SH NAME
msg \- send a brief message to other users
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBmsg \fIuser\fR
.br
\fBmsg \fIuser ... message\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
msg -- send a brief message to other users
Usage:	msg user
	msg user ... message
In the first form, send one line read from the standard input to 'user'.
In the second, send the argument 'message' to the list of users;
if 'message' is '-', the message is one line read from the standard input.
.HE
In the first form,
.B msg
reads one line from the standard input and sends it to
.IR user .
In the second form,
.B msg
sends the final argument,
.IR message ,
to every named
.I user
in the preceding list; if
.I message
is
.BR \- ,
.B msg
instead reads the message as one line from the standard input.
.PP
For each named
.IR user ,
.B msg
searches
.B /etc/utmp
to find the terminal on which
.I user
is logged in, then writes the message to that terminal, prefixed
with the sender's login name (or
.B daemon
if the sender is not logged in).
If a named
.I user
is not currently logged in,
.B msg
prints "\fIuser\fR: not logged in" and, unless invoked with
.BR \-s ,
sets a nonzero exit status.
.PP
Before writing to a terminal,
.B msg
checks whether the group/other execute bit of that terminal's device
file is set (the state controlled by
.BR mesg );
if it is not, an ordinary caller is refused, but the superuser is
asked whether to override the refusal.
A write that does not complete within ten seconds is abandoned, so
.B msg
cannot hang waiting on a terminal that is not reading its input.
.PP
If the caller is the superuser and the first argument is
.BR \-s ,
.B msg
suppresses the sender's login-name prefix on the message, closes its
standard error (so no diagnostics are printed), and forces a zero
exit status unless a real error (such as being unable to open
.BR /etc/utmp )
occurs.
This is intended for use by programs, such as
.BR rmail ,
that relay mail notifications through
.BR msg ,
and has no effect for an ordinary caller.
.SH FILES
.B /etc/utmp
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR mesg ,
.B write
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B msg
prints its usage message and exits with status 2 if called with no
arguments, exits with status 2 if
.B /etc/utmp
cannot be opened, and otherwise exits with status 1 if any named user
could not be reached (unless
.B \-s
was given).
.SH NOTES
.B msg
is normally installed set-user-id, so that its execute permission on
a terminal device can be used to allow or disallow use of the
terminal by other users, without granting general write access to
device files.
